# The emitted page only. backdrop.jpg beside it is a repo asset, not an artifact: §10's v0
# material alphas and blur spreads were judged against that photograph, and with the whole
# directory ignored the judgement was unreproducible off one machine — a fresh clone 404s the
# url() and grades every glass cell against the smooth gradient fallback instead, i.e. against
# exactly the easy backdrop §10 warns about.
packages/ui/preview/*.html
__screenshots__/
.vitest-attachments/
# Generated by Next on every command, and it OSCILLATES: `next dev` writes the .next/dev/types
# import, `next build` writes the .next/types one, so a tracked copy flips back and forth and
# shows as a modified file to whoever ran the other command last. It carries a do-not-edit
# header and nothing in CI reads it before a build (docs#lint dependsOn build), so it is an
# artifact, not a source file — and in a repo with drift laws, a tracked file that rewrites
# itself by command is a standing false positive.
apps/docs/next-env.d.ts

# Licensed fonts — NEVER commit these. Pangram Pangram's Font Starter Pack permits commercial
# use but forbids redistribution, and a public repo redistributes to everyone who clones it.
# The licence covers the deployed docs site, not the source tree. Contributors do not get the
# files; the stack falls back to system-ui for them, which is the package default anyway.
#
# Stated as the FILE TYPE rather than one vendor folder's name, because a name-shaped rule only
# protects the drop you thought of: the Neue Montreal folder was safe and a Neue York folder
# beside it would not have been. This repo tracks zero font binaries and ships none by design
# (§15 — the type layer owns family SLOTS, an app supplies the face), so the blanket rule
# forbids nothing the system wants. A font that ever needs committing is a decision, and
# `git add -f` is where that decision gets made explicitly.
*.woff
*.woff2
*.otf
*.ttf
*.eot
